The design frame is design principles **5.1** (a literal takes its type from context; a value is
never wrapped or cut to fit a type; a text is not a number), **10.6** (a value that cannot be
read is a located error that says what is wrong) and language reference **2.5** (the number
texts), with H15f's rule that every refusal uses a catalogue sentence.

---

## 1. Item 1 — the width a parameter is declared with

Before, the entry readers had one integer case (§`Int32`/`Int64` together) and one float case
(`Float32`/`Float64` together): the text was parsed as `int64_t`/`double` and then narrowed. So
`--arg n=3000000000` for `n: i32` was accepted (3000000000 → wrapped/cut into an int32), and a
refusal named `int`; an `f32` parameter was read as a double, so a value just above a float
midpoint could round twice, and a value outside the f32 range but inside the double range was
accepted and became `inf`/`0`.

Now the width is the declared one, at every path:

| path | code | change |
|---|---|---|
| `--arg` of an `i32`/`f32`/`int`/`float` parameter, interpreter | `src/execute/entry_args.cpp` | the combined cases are two templated helpers, `loadInteger<T>` (`parseWholeInteger<T>`) and `loadFloat<T>` (`parseWholeFloat<T>`), called with `T` = the declared width; the reason is `integerRangeReason<T>(target)` / `floatRangeReason<T>(target)`, and `target` is the declared type's name (`int`, `i32`, `float`, `f32`) |
| the same for `std::cpp`'s generated program | `include/camel/standalone/entry.h` | `EntryKind::Int32` / `Float32` read `Value::integer32` / `Value::number32` through `readInteger<T>` / `readNumber<T>` at that width; the `Int` / `Float` kinds are still `int64_t` / `double` |
| the sentences written into the generated program | `src/passes/trans/cpp/program_emitter.cpp` | `entryNumberText(TypeCode)` has one case per declared type, with that type's name and range; `entryFunction` emits `rt::EntryKind::Int32` / `Float32` and `args.integer32(i)` / `args.number32(i)` |
| each part of a `c64`/`c128` parameter | `src/execute/entry_args.cpp` (`complexFromText<float/double>`) | unchanged: a part was already read at the part's own width (H15f); the tests now lock the c64 boundary |

The messages, as they now read (interpreter; the nested clause is `Cannot bind the argument
'n' of 'main': …` at `main`, `RuntimeDiag::EntryArgumentInvalid`, `0x04000011`):

```text
error EntryArgumentInvalid: Cannot bind the argument 'n' of 'main': Cannot convert '3000000000' to i32: the value is outside the range of i32 (-2147483648 to 2147483647).
error EntryArgumentInvalid: Cannot bind the argument 'rate' of 'main': Cannot convert '3.5e38' to f32: the value is outside the range of f32 (at most about 3.4e38 in magnitude).
error EntryArgumentInvalid: Cannot bind the argument 'n' of 'main': Cannot convert the string 'three' to i32.
error EntryArgumentInvalid: Cannot bind the argument 'w' of 'main': Cannot convert '3.5e38+1i' to c64: the real part is outside the range of c64 (each part at most about 3.4e38 in magnitude).
```

The standalone program writes the same sentences itself (`EntryNumberText` in the generated
source), so the two agree byte for byte; `feat/trans/cpp.agree_with_fvm` runs the generated
programs against `std::fvm` (see §3).

A value that fits is converted **once**, at the declared width. `1.00000005960464479` is
`1 + 2^-24 + 1.4e-17`: a double rounds it to the float midpoint and a second narrowing rounds
to even, giving `1.0`; read once as a float it is the next float, `1.0000001`.
`entry_args.f32_once_at_width` locks that.

Boundary values covered (`test/cases/compile/entry_args/widths.cml`): `2147483647` and
`-2147483648` fit, `3000000000` and `-2147483649` are refused; `3.4028235e38` (the largest
f32) and `1.4e-45` (the smallest positive f32, a subnormal) fit, `3.5e38` and `1e-50` are
refused; `3.4028235e38+1i` is a c64 whose real part fits, `3.5e38+1i` and `1+3.5e38i` are
refused naming the real / imaginary part.

## 2. Item 2 — a text where a number is declared is a type mismatch

No implicit text-to-number conversion (5.1), so there are two situations and two existing
codes; no new code was invented:

| situation | code | message |
|---|---|---|
| a text where a number is **declared** (`let`/`var`, a struct field, an array element, a generic body instantiated with a number type) | `SemanticDiag::DeclaredTypeMismatch` (`0x03000110`, existed and already meant this) | `This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'int'.` |
| an explicit cast `'42' as int` | `SemanticDiag::DynamicCastForbidden` (`0x03000017`, existed and is what the computed `s as int` reports) | `Dynamic cast from 'string' to 'int' is not allowed.` |

Both help texts were extended with the conversion to write instead: `int(s)` / `parse_int(s)` for
a text, `float(s)` / `parse_float(s)` for a float, `float(n)` for a number of another type,
`v as T` for a module type.

Decided in:

- GCT, `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`: `reportLiteralCastFailure` takes a trailing
  `bool explicitCast`; when the literal's data is a `StringData` it commits
  `DynamicCastForbidden` from `'string'` (for the `As` branch) or `DeclaredTypeMismatch` (for
  `declaredValue` / `literalNodeInType`), and no longer says the text is not a number. The `As`
  branch passes `/*explicitCast=*/true`.
- GIR, `src/compile/gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp`: `Builder::rejectLiteralData`'s `StringData`
  branch reports `DeclaredTypeMismatch` too (it is the defensive path for a declared position
  that reaches GIR with a type variable, e.g. a generic body).

The conversion calls are unchanged on purpose: `int('12abc')` is still
`SemanticDiag::LiteralNotRepresentable` with the reason `the text is not a number`, and
`int('999…')` / `float('1e999')` still say out of range — those are conversions, where running
the numeric rule on the literal is exactly the point (H15f, `literal_context.plan.toml`).

Neighbours checked by hand and locked by tests where they are cheap:

| source | target | diagnostic |
|---|---|---|
| `let x: float = '1.5'` | `float` | `DeclaredTypeMismatch` (`'string'`, `'float'`) |
| `let z: c128 = '1+2i'` | `c128` | `DeclaredTypeMismatch` (`'string'`, `'c128'`) |
| `let x: bool = 'true'` | `bool` | `DeclaredTypeMismatch` (`'string'`, `'bool'`) |
| `let x: i32 = '42'` | `i32` | `DeclaredTypeMismatch` (`'string'`, `'i32'`) |
| `'42' as int` / `as float` / `as f32` / `as c128` / `as bool` | | `DynamicCastForbidden` |
| `let x: int? = '42'` | `int?` | `DynamicCastForbidden` (unchanged: the tagged path already said it) |
| `int('12abc')`, `float('1.5x')` | | `LiteralNotRepresentable`, `the text is not a number` (unchanged) |
| `['1']` where `int[]` is declared | | `ElementTypeMismatch` (existing; already a type mismatch) |

